import "core"
import rss("artifacts/xkcd.xml")


# Tests importing an RSS feed. The feed is a page and each entry of it is a value declared on that
  page, so a feed of four entries is a page of four declarations beneath what the feed calls itself.
  The feed is named as a file rather than as an address, which is why there is a golden to compare
  against at all: a feed asked of the network would be a test whose answer was whatever somebody
  else had published that morning, and a feed read from a file is read the same way every time and
  records nothing. What the address form does that this does not is ask, and keep what it was told —
  which @test/Syntax/RssCacheTests.hs@ holds it to, with the asking stubbed out.
  The page names the module declaring what an entry is, rather than being given it with the core
  every page is given, so this reaches @stack/rss/main.ct@ and writes down what that is read as too.
main{io -> IO}= io.exit(val= 0)
